    {
      "tag": "Subordinators",
      "h2": "Joining unequal parts",
      "body_html": "<p>A <strong>subordinating conjunction</strong> joins a main clause to a dependent one: 'I ran <em>because</em> I was late.'</p>"
    },
    {
      "tag": "ABCWWWWHIIT",
      "h2": "A memorable list",
      "body_html": "<p>The mnemonic <strong>ABCWWWWHIIT</strong> covers: After, Although, Because, Before, When, Whenever, While, Where, How, If, In case, That. Many start a subordinate clause.</p>"
    },
    {
      "tag": "Complex sentences",
      "h2": "Building depth",
      "body_html": "<p>A <strong>complex sentence</strong> has one main clause and at least one subordinate clause: '<em>Although it rained</em>, we kept walking.'</p>"
    }
